# {{ .prefix }}-executing-plans

<!-- awf:section positioning -->
The inline implementation chain iterates plan phases. Inline is selected per phase, so a mixed plan can hand subagent-driven phases to its companion.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Invoke for an accepted plan with an `inline` phase. Read every phase's declared execution mode; do not infer one mode for the whole plan.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Procedure

<!-- awf:section procedure-resolve-plan -->
1. Resolve the mutable plan and read its complete phase structure. Before mutation, validate the one immutable effort slug and exact `.awf/efforts/<slug>/memory.md` path, confirm `Effort: <slug>`, and preserve one user-managed writer. Planned work is concrete and non-minimal, the effort always owns memory, repository sources and current-state documentation outrank checkpoint prose, and standalone memory is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-raise-concerns -->
2. You, the parent executing this plan, raise missing phase context, ownership, paths, checks, or closing subject with the user before editing.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-per-task -->
3. **Iterate phases, not tasks.** Per `{{ .layout.maintainableCodeDesign }}`, you preserve the plan's settled structural choices, including a bounded enabling refactor; reassess if grounded source contradicts them, stop rather than bolt correctness onto the wrong abstraction, and do not drift from the plan. No drift from the plan. You run `awf context <the phase's exact paths>` before editing.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
For an `inline` phase, the parent owns every ordered task, integration, staged check, gate, phase-closing commit, report-only phase review, focused settlement commits, and checkpoint. A batch may dispatch explicitly partitioned sequential commit-disabled helpers only; each is the `implementer` agent in its commit-disabled mode, whose rendered contract governs its path confinement, green obligation, escalation, and return shape. Each brief carries the effort slug and exact owned memory path as read-only context, and helpers never edit shared memory: {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}choose the smallest model expected to complete reliably: `small` is for narrow, mechanical, low-ambiguity work; `standard` is for substantive but bounded work; and `large` is for broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence work. Uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope requires reconsideration and possible escalation. Omit the `model` field entirely to use configured role routing; when the selected complexity warrants an override, pass the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value.{{ else }}choose the smallest model expected to complete reliably: `small` is for narrow, mechanical, low-ambiguity work; `standard` is for substantive but bounded work; and `large` is for broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence work. Uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope requires reconsideration and possible escalation. Select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly; if this harness cannot select a model, use its default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable.{{ end }} You inventory each return, reject out-of-subset writes, retain shared-file ownership, run the deterministic post-check, and never delegate the phase commit. Stage the complete transaction, run `awf check --staged`, then {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }}. Commit only after both commands pass; the hook repeats the staged check as defense in depth. Review settles before checkpointing.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-routine -->

If an implementer stops dirty, first inventory `git status --short`, diff, completed and remaining work, prior concerns, and failed checks. Choose exactly one: complete inline; restore the known green baseline and restart the complete revised phase; or stop for required user input. An explicit ownership transfer includes the complete revised phase, inventory, completed and remaining work, prior concerns, and recovery verification. Never issue a blind successor instruction for one task or dispatch over a dirty checkout.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section tdd-opt-in -->
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section gate-tier-detail -->
{{ with .data.e2eSuitePaths }}Use the fuller gate when a phase touches a declared end-to-end surface: {{ range . }}`{{ .path }}` {{ end }}.{{ end }}
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-adr-final-commit -->
4. Apply V2 operation batches atomically with their matching claims and lifecycle event in the owning phase.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-non-adr-final-commit -->
5. Freeze a plan only in its declared final transaction after review settlement.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
6. After all settled phases, invoke `{{ .prefix }}-reviewing-impl`.
<!-- awf:end -->

## Notes
<!-- awf:section project-invariants -->
- Expected output is non-negotiable. Never weaken assertions or adjust a golden merely to pass.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-gate -->
- Gates are mandatory; fix the root cause.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-auto-commit -->
- Auto-commit the phase only when green.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-one-concern -->
- One coherent phase concern per closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-docs-travel -->
- Docs travel with behavior.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section red-flags -->
## Red flags

Do not turn ordered tasks into transaction boundaries, let helpers commit, or checkpoint before report-only review settlement.
<!-- awf:end -->
